# C# Coding Conventions

- Target the existing TFM and package versions already referenced by the project; do not bump casually.
- Enable nullable reference types; prefer `string?` / `T?` over null-forgiving unless matching an existing seam.
- Prefer `sealed` for concrete model/result types that are not designed for inheritance.
- Use expression-bodied members for trivial one-liners when the surrounding file already does.
- Guard public entry points with argument validation (`?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(...))`, range checks).
- Keep library code free of test-framework references; test helpers live in `TestUtilities*`.
- Match existing formatting: aligned assignments when nearby code aligns them; file-scoped namespaces are not used here—follow the file you edit.
